Deathkin wrote:
>
> I was looking into the vagaries of puddings dividing when hit with
> iron weapons, using wizard mode and a polearm.  Interestingly, the
> puddings would only divide when I meleed them with the polearm, but
> never did when (a)pplying from a distance.  Sourcediving into the
> vagaries of polearm usage revealed that use_pole() in src/apply.c
> calls thitmonst() in src/dothrow.c, which in turn calls hmon() in
> src/uhitm.c with thrown = 1.  (a)pplied polearms are treated as thrown
> attacks.

It may be because the range isn't one, but applied polearms should
be treated as melee for all purposes that I can think of.  It should
even trigger defensive attacks some of which should fail for lack of
being at range one.

> This may not be a bug, owing to some (extremely odd) reasoning of how
> polearms are supposed to work; however, my own thinking is that
> (a)pplying a polearm is the modus operandi, and meleeing with it would
> generally be more equivalent to whacking with a quarterstaff, as the
> distance disallows the leverage for swinging; based on
> <http://hea-www.harvard.edu/~fine/Fun/polearms.html>, it seems the
> general idea is slashing weapons at a distance.

Melee at range one means waving the blade of the polearm well
past the monster and whacking it with the length of the pole.
Since the power of quarterstaff is in the leverage available at the
end of the staff not along its length, the comparison should be
with using a bow in melee.  Sure enough the damage done is
like a bow.

> Which makes me wonder why using them at a distance doesn't divide
> puddings.  It clearly fits the logic for a polearm.  I know NetHack Is
> Not Real Life; however, this is exactly puddings should divide when
> hit by (a)pplied polearms!  It is easy to see why this is not the case
> in the code, as they are treated as thrown weapons; however, I can
> find the issue addressed nowhere, not in the code comments nor
> anywhere on rgrn.

Does seem like a bug to me.



